7 species in the Nordic countries. L. edodes (Berk.) Sing., an Asian species (shiitake), is cultivated for food in Sand F. D&D 23.
Lit.: Pegler 1975, 1983, Pilat 1935, 1946, Redhead & Ginns 1985.

1,0 $ Cap and stem whitish to ochraceous, velvety-felty, becoming viscid from a brown resinous coating; cap 3-5 cm; gills white to ochraceous, decurrent. Sp 7-10 x 2.5-3 µm   ; metuloids present. - On conifers. ///KM 314, MH III 100, Bres 512./// F: ? $ L. adhaerens (Alb. & Schw. Fr.) Fr. (Neolentinus a. (Alb. & Schw. Fr.) Redhead & Ginns)
1,2 $ Cap dry
1,0$ generalites///7 species in the Nordic countries. L. edodes (Berk.) Sing., an Asian species (shiitake), is cultivated for food in Sand F. D&D 23.
1,0$ bibliographie///Pegler 1975, 1983, Pilat 1935, 1946, Redhead & Ginns 1985.

 
2,3 $ Cap smooth
2,4 $ Cap hairy or scaly
3,0 $ Gills finely dentate. Metuloids absent. Cap 1.5-5(-11) cm, yellow to ochraceous, later whitish, reniform to cupulate; gills white to yellowish, anastomosing; stem very short or absent, central to eccentric; sp-print pale ochraceous; smell strong of aniseed. Sp 7-8 x 3.5-4 µm  ///- Solitary on trunks of Salix; autumn. ///D&D 17, M&J I  Panus 1. ///D: Elyl (Silkeborg, Rold Skov). N: 1-2 s r. S: 1-3 r. F: 2-3 V, EH, OP. $ L. suavissimus Fr. (Panus s. (Fr.) Sing.)
3,0 $ Gills with entire edge. Metuloids numerous, clavate to ventricose. Cap 4-8 cm, infundibuliform or irregularly lobed, wood brown to pale tan, at first faintly purplish lilac; gills reddish to wood brown, anastomosing; stem short, eccentric to lateral, at the base tomentose lilac, then pale tan. Sp 6-7 x 3-3.5 µm    (Fig. 2). ///- Subsolitary or clustered on stumps and branches of deciduous trees; summer or late autumn. D&D 20, Phil 187, R&H 207. ///D: o. N: l-40-r. S: 1-4 c-o. F: 2-3 n-KiL c-o. $ L. conchatus (Bull. : Fr.) Schroet. (Panus c. (Bull. : Fr.) Fr., L. torulosus (Pers. : Fr.) Lloyd)
4,0 $ Gill edge entire, stem rudimentary. Metuloids numerous. Cap hirsute strigose, 2-5 cm, reniform to infundibuliform, at first vinaceous, later ochraceous, edge incurved; gills at first vinaceous, later ochraceous; stem obconical, central to lateral, hirsute; sp-print ochraceous; taste acrid. Sp 5-6 x 2 µm   . - On stumps and branches of Fagus and other deciduous trees; late autumn. ///MH III 94, Bres 519, C 1122. ///D:? S:! $ L. strigosus (Schw.) Fr. (Panus rudis Fr.)
4,5 $ Gill edge serrate or entire. Metuloids scattered or absent
5,0 $ On living trees, stumps and construction timber of conifers. Cap 5-15 cm, yellowish white, with large, umber, appressed scales; gills serrate, sinuate to decurrent; stem central to lateral; veil indistinct; smell strong, aromatic. Sp 8-14 x 3-3.5 µm   . ///- Summer to autumn. ///MLge, R&H 208, D&D 22. /// D: r. I: SA (Kvisker) on imported wood. N: 1-4 c. S: 1-3 c. F: 2-3 n~InL c-o. $ L. lepideus (Fr. : Fr.) Fr. (Neolentinus I. (Fr. : Fr.) Redhead & Ginns)
5,6 $ On living or dead deciduous trees, mainly Populus and Salix; gills strongly decurrent
6,0 $ Cap and stem with dark brown scales; gill edge serrate; cap 4-8 cm, umbilicate to infundibuliform, whitish with small, fibrous, adhering fuscous black scales; gills white to yellowish; stem central to eccentric, with fine brown scales and a fugacious veil. Sp 8-10 x 2-2.5 µm   . ///- Summer. ///D&D 21, MH III 95, Ri 26,2. ///D: Sj (Gentofte, Glreno). S: 1 Sk. $ L. tigrinus (Bull. : Fr.) Fr. (Panus t. (Bull. : Fr.) Sing.)
6,0 $ Cap and stem not dark scaly; gill edge entire or finely denticulate. Cap 5-15 cm, yellowish white, with minute reddish brown scales; gills initially venose, becoming lamellate; stem central to slightly eccentric, felty-scaly; veil absent. Sp 10-14 x 3.5-5 µm   . ///- Mainly on Populus; summer to early autumn. ///Bres 511, C 693. ///D: Loll (Hovengeskoven). S:! $ L. eyathiformis (Schaeff.) Bres. (L. schaefferi (Weinm.) Kaarik, Neolentinus s. (Weinm.) Redhead & Ginns)
